# Ceremony revision 4 — Codex confirmation 3

Reviewed 2026-11-06 against `BRIEF-CONFIRM-3.md`, including its four owner prechecks. **Do not run rehearsal 2 as written: it returns NUC-exposed media to the signing environment (HIGH).** F01's replacement command preserves the policy byte on the inspected upstream sources, but its printed expected output needs correction. F03's fresh-boot assignments are fixed. Real key generation remains gated on the corrected rehearsal and its actual-stack results.

Snapshot: HEAD `2262a118a8fa16d06161d75a5bfa75b0b0a607ce`; `docs/ceremony/CEREMONY.md` SHA-256 `10c3f915c902b6ba1dfd48b70810e0562455bacab26a5644419269ba8bb1d876`. Unqualified line references below mean that file. Repository access was read-only except this report. The pre-existing untracked brief and log were preserved. No keys, cards, backup devices, PINs or secrets were accessed. Validation consists of document tracing and upstream source inspection, not a hardware rehearsal or production authorization.

## F01 — raw policy survives; expected spelling is wrong (MEDIUM)

**Lines 169–176,284–285.** Yes: `SCD GETATTR UIF-1` bypasses the lossy `gpg --card-status` parser and conveys the DO D6 value without collapsing 0/1/2. No: for bytes `02 20`, the inspected status serializer prints **`S UIF-1 %02+`**, not `%02%20`. This is reversible status encoding, not literal unescaped bytes.

The source chain, using upstream GnuPG tag `gnupg-2.4.8` (also inspected the corresponding 2.4.4 path):

- [`scd/app-openpgp.c:1175–1177`](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/app-openpgp.c#L1175-L1177) maps UIF-1/2/3 to D6/D7/D8 with special handling zero. At [1290](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/app-openpgp.c#L1290), `get_one_do` obtains the value; the default branch at [1413–1416](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/app-openpgp.c#L1413-L1416) passes `value,valuelen` to `send_status_info`. No boolean conversion occurs.
- [`get_one_do`, lines 613–692](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/app-openpgp.c#L613-L692), extracts the DO content from its enclosing object or retrieves the individual object; it preserves content and length. This can use the scdaemon cache, so “raw” does not mean a mandatory direct APDU on every call. The setter invalidates its cache before writing at [3117–3121](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/app-openpgp.c#L3117-L3121); cache removal also follows the enclosing tag at [570–576](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/app-openpgp.c#L570-L576). The ordinary set-then-get sequence is intended to reread card state.
- [`scd/command.c:2625–2641`](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/scd/command.c#L2625-L2641) percent-escapes control bytes and literal plus/quote/percent, but encodes a space byte as `+`. Thus `02` becomes `%02`, and `20` becomes `+`.
- [`agent/call-scd.c:1139–1155`](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/agent/call-scd.c#L1139-L1155) forwards this status without policy interpretation. [`tools/gpg-connect-agent.c:2217–2235`](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/tools/gpg-connect-agent.c#L2217-L2235) prints status and OK lines. It does not turn the plus into `%20`.
- [`g10/card-util.c:2250–2260`](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.8/g10/card-util.c#L2250-L2260) sets `permanent` to first byte `0x02`, `on` to `0x01`, `off` to zero, and the second byte to `0x20`.

Expected relevant successful output after setting permanent:

```
S UIF-1 %02+
OK
```

Rehearsal 2 should record the complete command output, Tails/GnuPG/scdaemon versions, setter errors if any, and the identity of C. A successful raw result decodes to `02 20`; `%01+` or `%00+` fails the required policy. ERR, missing/empty UIF status, malformed/unexpected length or bytes also leave confirmation unestablished: stop and investigate. If the actual stack prints `%02%20`, that contradicts this inspected serializer's spelling prediction, although a correctly decoded value would still be `02 20`; identify the actual version/patch before changing the documented expectation. A no-touch signature succeeding falsifies enforcement even with a nominal `02` readback. A correct readback alone does not establish PIN policy, trusted firmware or host integrity.

Disposition: **the previous HIGH loss-of-distinction defect is resolved at source level; actual-stack confirmation remains pending.** Correct line 174 before rehearsal. Because line 175 tests the first byte, the spelling error does not by itself authorize accepting ordinary touch as permanent; it can confuse or stop a correct run. These two inspected tags do not establish behavior of every 2.4 release or downstream build.

## F03 — resolved for the reported omission

**Lines 194–210.** Both fresh backup boots and the separate card-only boot now explicitly assign `B` and `FPR`. The card-only block imports only `public.asc`. Under the stated fresh home and absent secret import, a successful signature exercises the card rather than a leftover generation key. Substitute the mounted label for B, including the release labels for key B. This is a procedural repair; no card-only test was executed here.

## H01 — rehearsal returns a NUC-exposed stick to a signing session (HIGH)

**Lines 281–283 versus 31–33, and `docs/ceremony/2026-11-01-rehearsal-1.md:17,28`.** The latter record explicitly says the rehearsal-1 transfer stick touched the NUC and must never return to a signing session. Rehearsal 2 nevertheless names that exact stick as its out stick. This is an actual recorded crossing, not speculation that it might have visited the NUC.

The dress rehearsal connects that medium to the same trusted signing hardware and session that handles the two future ROOT backup sticks. A throwaway key limits the value of that key; it does not make parsing a returned device/filesystem safe for the host or attached media. No-autorun is useful but does not remove this exposure. A later Tails reboot or formatting A's backup files is not proof that compromised firmware or devices have become trusted again. This is a boundary violation, not evidence that compromise has occurred.

Use a separate, new rehearsal out stick that has never touched the NUC. It may be ejected and reinserted within the offline rehearsal to collect S9 files, then goes out once and never returns after NUC contact. Reserve both new real out sticks for their real sessions. This requires another clean stick beyond the six assigned at 292–299, unless a distinct never-NUC spare is already available. If rehearsal 2 has already used the old stick, record the deviation and obtain an explicit disposition of affected hardware/media before real key generation; this review cannot certify them clean.

The HIGH classification is against the ceremony's own trusted-session/media boundary, not a newly invented registered ban on a particular USB vendor. The registered production procedure must control media crossings (A10.4.2); this rehearsal does not discharge that production gate.

## Other dress-rehearsal and media findings

- **A's backup sticks: conditionally suitable.** Lines 281–282 explicitly require reformatting them at real S5. Under the trusted-session assumption, creating throwaway LUKS backups does not consume them or prevent later use; fresh formatting, new real passphrase, exports and both restore tests are required. Formatting destroys usable rehearsal volumes, not necessarily every physical trace; the surviving key would be throwaway. Do not reuse the rehearsal passphrase or mistake its files/fingerprint for A's. H01 must be repaired first. B's different sticks and A/B cards are not tested by C's run; their own real-session S0–S9/Session 2 checks remain mandatory.
- **Output destination preparation gap (MEDIUM): lines 33,145–153,188,283,298–299.** The real out sticks are new, while the rehearsal uses an already prepared one. No step formats/mounts/checks the out volume or checks that O is its actual mount. A new stick may be unformatted or have a different label. If the directory exists without the expected mount, export could go to the wrong filesystem. Add preparation for a public output volume and an exact mount check before export/copy; rehearse it on the new rehearsal out stick. At S9 explicitly reinsert/mount the same offline out stick, check O again, copy both proof files, sync and eject. It must not visit the NUC between S6 and S9. Existing S6 checks test the exported file, not the identity of the mounted device; S9 has no printed copy/check commands.
- **S9 command gap persists (MEDIUM): lines 184–186.** “Run the signing command again” still relies on the operator inserting the alternate `--output` paths. Print the full commands for `nt.asc` and `p2.asc`, require a fresh/nonexistent no-touch output and confirm no usable signature after an actual timeout, then verify `p2.asc`. Cancelling an overwrite prompt or an unrelated failure is not an enforcement test. Acceptance at 214–215 already requires expected behavior, so this is a reproducibility/false-observation risk, not permission to count any error as success.
- **Scope and cleanup: lines 278–288.** Running all S0–S10 and three fresh test boots exercises the main generation, recovery and card paths, provided the above gaps are repaired and deviations recorded. Reset C only after those tests and recording their outcomes; never extend that reset instruction to A/B. C is disposable, so this reset does not authorize destroying a real remaining copy. The rehearsal has one key/session cycle; it does not exercise two-key physical segregation, actual final storage, every failure branch, production release signing/acquisition or TEST-authority rejection. Those omissions do not invalidate a bounded key-generation rehearsal, but must not be reported as exercised.
- **Vendor wording (LOW): line 301.** Mixed vendors reduce some common-batch exposure; they do not establish that one defect cannot affect both (shared components/controllers remain possible). Replace the absolute “cannot” with a bounded risk-reduction statement.
- **Historical summaries:** line 268 still describes the rejected colon-value advice. Label it superseded by revision 4 so it cannot be used as the current S8 instruction. The blanket shutdown summary at 269–270 still has pre-generation and total-loss exceptions in the failure table. Neither overrides the operative steps.

No further HIGH finding was identified in this bounded revision-4 review. Earlier custody-record completeness residuals (actual firmware and full export/KDF settings) are not discharged merely by this confirmation; the draft still needs its own conformance review.

## Appended owner prechecks

| Check | Result | File:line evidence and reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Capture row | **PASS** | `docs/ceremony/CEREMONY.md:18–24,245–250,264` assigns authority roles and expressly recognizes that DNS+repository capture can substitute both bootstrap publications; it promises neither rejection of fresh attacker-key receipts nor capture visibility. Ground truth `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md:34–37,50–53,72–74`: attacker-key receipts pass under dual capture, honest-material protection is conditional, release compromise is a separate distribution failure. H01 does not change those receipt claims. |
| 2. Trigger clause | **PASS** | `docs/ceremony/CEREMONY.md:9–11,20–21,245–250` cites independence as a prerequisite and defers production use. `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md:624–643,668–676` registers that prerequisite, the before-first-public-release gate and later bootstrap record. Generating keys or rehearsing C does not assert that the release trigger occurred, invoke fallback or discharge independence. H01 is a violation of the proposed procedure's boundary, not proof the registered fallback trigger occurred. |
| 3. Requester | **PASS (bounded key-generation claim)** | `docs/ceremony/CEREMONY.md:9–11,40–43,53–55,178–188,264` gives ceremony operations to the author, recognizes exportable backup authority and expressly defers independent production request approval. A trusted session/host can submit signing requests; PIN/touch do not authenticate the meaning of those bytes. An unlocked secret backup permits software signing without the token. Production requests originate with the owner on the NUC and require the author's independent exact-hash approval under `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md:251–278`. Storage separation is not claimed to establish that requester independence. |
